"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the plan and wants the full design (stage=result). Let me produce a complete experiment design with all 5 points, plus an uncertainties section. No execution needed. Let me make it concrete and complete.

Let me fill in the concrete model choice: a small LLaMA-style decoder. Options: a LLaMA-60M style (like the "TinyLlama-60M"-ish or the 60M variant used in many papers), LLaMA-110M, GPT-2 small (124M). The BAdam paper used LLaMA2-7B; a scaled-down analog would be a small LLaMA-style decoder. Given CPU feasibility, I'll pick a ~60M or ~110M LLaMA-style decoder, 12 layers, hidden 768, 12 heads, intermediate 2048. Let me design a concrete one:

Option: "LLaMA-60M-scale": 
- vocab ~32,000 (LLaMA vocab), or reduce to keep it cheap. Actually token embedding is 32000×768 ≈ 25M params. Total ~60M.
- layers: 12, hidden dim 768, num heads 12, head dim 64, intermediate 2048 (3× factor), gated MLP (up, gate project to 2048, down back to 768), RoPE, RMSNorm, no bias (LLaMA has no bias terms — good, excludes bias automatically; only 2D matrices).
- Params: embedding 32000×768 = 24.6M; per layer: q 768×768=0.59M, k 0.59M, v 0.59M, o 0.59M, up 768×2048=1.57M, gate 1.57M, down 2048×768=1.57M ⇒ ~7.1M/layer ×12 ≈ 85M + embedding 24.6M + lm_head (tied or untied?). If we tie embedding and lm_head, 24.6M reused. Total ≈ 24.6 + 85 ≈ 110M. Hmm that gives ~110M. To hit ~60-80M, could use hidden 512, intermediate 1376, 8 heads, 10-12 layers, vocab 32000.

Actually let me be careful and give a concrete config with a clear parameter count. Let me choose a config that's clearly ~60–125M:
- Model: LLaMA-style decoder, d_model=768, n_heads=12, head_dim=64, n_layers=12, intermediate_size=2048, vocab=32000, tie_word_embeddings=True (llama2 does not tie? actually LLaMA does not tie embeddings; but to save memory we can tie or not—for finetuning either is fine). Untied lm_head: 32000×768 = 24.6M; embedding 24.6M; per-layer attention: q,k,v,o each 768×768=589,824 ⇒ 4×0.59 = 2.36M; mlp up/gate 768×2048 each = 1.57M ×2 = 3.15M; down 2048×768 = 1.57M; total per layer ≈ 7.08M; ×12 = 85M; + emb 24.6M + head 24.6M = 134M. Slightly above 125M. With tied embeddings: 24.6 + 85 + 24.6(lm_head if untied) — tied → lm_head shares embedding, so total = 24.6 + 85 = 109.6M. Good: ~110M with tied embeddings, within 60–125M. And bias=None (LLaMA has no biases) → cleanly only 2D matrices.

Alternatively pick a slightly smaller config: 8 layers, hidden 768, intermediate 2048, vocab 32000, tied emb: emb 24.6M + 8×7.08 = 56.6 + 24.6 = 81M. That's ~80M, comfortably cheap. Let me just present one concrete config and compute params. I'll go with:

**Concrete model (TINY-LLaMA-110M-ish)**:
- architecture: decoder-only, LLaMA-style (RoPE, RMSNorm, SwiGLU gated MLP), no bias anywhere.
- d_model = 768; n_layers = 12; n_heads = 12; head_dim = 64; intermediate = 2048; vocab = 32000; tie_word_embeddings = True; seq_len = 256; max_position_embeddings = 512.
- Params ≈ embedding 24.6M + lm_head 0 (tied) + 12 × (attn 2.36M + mlp 4.72M) = 24.6M + 12×7.08M ≈ 109.6M.
- 2D matrix list per layer: q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj (all 768×768); up_proj, gate_proj (768×2048); down_proj (2048×768) → 7 matrices × 12 layers = 84 matrices + optionally token_embedding (32000×768) and lm_head (tied → same matrix, count once) → 86 module matrices analyzed. Excluded: embedding & lm_head in the primary module-set? The researcher asked to analyze q/k/v/o/up/gate/down and clarify exclusion of embeddings/bias/LN. So primary module set = the 7 per-layer matrices (84 matrices); embeddings/lm_head/RMSNorm/bias explicitly excluded (RMSNorm and biases are 1D; embeddings are huge-vocab 2D but not the target; we can mention optional secondary analysis of embedding, but primary set excludes it). I'll state exclusion clearly.

**Dataset/task**: To make finetuning meaningful and CPU-tractable: a decoder LM finetuning task on a small slice. Options: WikiText-2 (~2M tokens) or a subset of The Pile/OpenWebText. BAdam original uses instruction-following (Alpaca) + GSM8K. At our scale, choose a lightweight supervised finetuning: e.g., WikiText-2 subset of ~1–2M tokens for causal LM, 1–3 epochs. Or Alpaca-style SFT. I'll pick a causal-LM SFT on a contiguous 1.5M-token slice of an open text corpus (e.g., Wikitext-103 subset or a synthetic local slice) to keep it deterministic and small; but since we can't download, note the dataset would be loaded from a local file in a real run. Let me propose: task = next-token prediction on a fixed 1M-token slice of WikiText-2 (or OpenWebText), seq_len 256, batch 16 → ~ (1e6/256)/16 ≈ 244 steps/epoch; 3 epochs ≈ 732 steps. Very CPU-cheap. Good: update count manageable.

Let me compute: tokens per epoch = 1,000,000; tokens per step = 16×256 = 4096; steps/epoch = 244; 3 epochs = 732 optimizer steps (Adam: each module updated 732 times). For BAdam with K blocks: each block active every K steps; a module in block k gets active steps ≈ 732/K per epoch-consistent run. For K=2: ~366; K=4: ~183; K=12: ~61. That's the "equal forward passes" primary convention. Secondary convention: equal per-block active steps: run T_Badam = K × T_Adam steps (same active steps per module as Adam, but K× more forward passes); gives each module ~732 active steps, matched to Adam.

**Optimizer/schedule**: AdamW, lr=2e-5, β1=0.9, β2=0.999, ε=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0 or 0.01. For LLaMA-style SFT, wd=0.0 common; but AdamW with wd=0.01 adds a regularization that scales spectra. I'll pick wd=0.0 for the primary experiment to keep the "perturbation = accumulated update" identity clean and avoid decay confounding the spectrum shape; optionally a secondary sweep. Actually AdamW update includes decoupled wd: W ← W − η(m̂/(sqrt(v̂)+ε)) − η·λ·W. Then Σ updates = W_T − W_0 still holds exactly (each step is an additive update). But the wd term is proportional to current W, coupling spectrum. To keep the design clean and answer "does BAdam's block structure produce comparable shape", I'll set wd=0 (weight decay 0) as primary, note wd=0.01 as a robustness secondary. LR schedule: cosine with warmup (e.g., 5% warmup steps), same schedule object for all arms, schedule stepping per optimizer step. To keep Adam vs BAdam "comparable" even though BAdam's active steps differ in count, and since in the primary convention we use equal forward passes (T_Adam = 732, T_Badam = 732 total global steps for both), the schedule is defined over global step index 0..731 and is identical across arms. That's clean: same global schedule, same epochs. In secondary convention (equal per-block active steps), T_Badam = K×732 global steps; schedule extended over K×732 steps — this is a different schedule; we note it isolates block-update-count but conflates schedule length (mitigate by also trying a schedule that restarts per cycle — or just report both and acknowledge).

Actually, to make secondary cleaner, we could keep schedule defined per-active-step rather than per-global-step, and pad inactive steps (freeze optimizer at frozen blocks) — but in BAdam the LR is applied only on active steps. The BAdam paper applies schedule per iteration globally. I'll document this as an explicit modeling choice with the acknowledged confound, and mitigate by reporting schedule-curve + final-LR matched.

**Seeds**: 5 seeds {0,1,2,3,4}. Grid: arms = {Adam} ∪ {BAdam, K=2,4,12} = 4 optimizer configs × 5 seeds = 20 runs. Plus optional secondary convention runs. 20 runs × 732 steps on ~110M model — feasible on 224 CPUs in maybe hours (each run ~1-2h). That's fine to state as an estimate.

**Pre-registered core measures & thresholds** (this is requested #2):

Definitions:
- Learning perturbation per module m: ΔW_m = W_T^m − W_0^m, SVD → singular values {s_i}, sorted descending.
- Heavy-tailed measures:
  • rank–frequency OLS: fit log s_i = c − a log i on top-k tail with k = min(⌈0.3·d⌉, 200); α_RF = a.
  • Hill: α_H(k) = k / Σ_{i=1}^k log(s_i/s_{k+1}), k plateau-selected (sweep k from 10 to 0.5·d; pick k where α_H(k) is most stable — smallest local slope; report selected k).
  • Pareto MLE with x_min (Clauset–Shalizi–Newman): α_P = 1 + n / Σ_i log(s_i / x_min), x_min chosen to minimize KS distance between empirical and fitted CDF in s ≥ x_min.
  • Report α = median of the three estimators? Or primary = Pareto-MLE α_P (most standard), with Hill & OLS as robustness. I'll make α_Primary = α_P (Plackett/Clauset MLE), report α_H and α_RF in supplementary. Bootstrap 95% CI = BCa or percentile over B=1000 resamples of seeds × modules within a type.
- Effective rank:
  • PR = (Σ s_i)² / Σ s_i².
  • r_τ via normalized cumulative squared-energy: smallest r with (Σ_{i≤r} s_i²)/(Σ s_i²) ≥ τ, τ∈{0.9,0.99}; report both normalized r_τ/d.
- "Comparable" (pre-registered, primary decision rule):
  For each module m the descriptors (r_0.9/d or PR, α_P) are paired across arms built from the same seed:
  (i) Effective rank ratio ρ_m = rB/rA ∈ [0.80, 1.25] (i.e., within factor 1.25 in both directions, equally on log scale) — "comparable rank."
  (ii) Tail index: 95% bootstrap CIs of α_P for BAdam and Adam for module m overlap ("comparable tail").
  Global answer rule (pre-registered):
  • YES (BAdam comparable to Adam) iff: median_m ρ_m ∈ [0.80,1.25] AND fraction of modules with per-module tail-CI overlap ≥ 80% AND fraction of modules with ρ_m ∈ [0.80,1.25] ≥ 80% — all three conditions must hold.
  • NO if any condition fails (or if ≥20% of modules violate).
  • Additional reporting without a verdict: per-module-type medians (q, k, v, o, up, gate, down), per-layer curves, |Δα| distribution, and number of spiked components above MP bulk edge.
  Equivalence backup: Welch/TOST-style two-one-sided test on log(ρ) with bounds ±log(1.25) for each module type, reported as p-values (descriptive only, since modules aren't i.i.d. samples).
