 rule, not an absolute heavy-ness threshold).\n\n2.3 Effective rank measures (same SVD):\n- Participation ratio PR = (\u03a3 s_i)\u00b2 / \u03a3 s_i\u00b2.\n- Variance-threshold rank r_\u03c4 = smallest r with (\u03a3_{i\u2264r} s_i\u00b2)/(\u03a3 s_i\u00b2) \u2265 \u03c4, \u03c4 \u2208 {0.9, 0.99}; report normalized r_\u03c4/d so values are comparable across 768\u00d7768 and 768\u00d72048 modules.\n\n2.4 \"Comparable\" \u2014 pre-registered decision rule (primary, set before running):\nFor each module m we pair the same-seed, same-module descriptors across arms.\n(i) Effective-rank ratio: \u03c1_m = r(m; BAdam)/r(m; Adam) (for r = r_0.9/d; also report PR-ratio). Call m rank-comparable iff \u03c1_m \u2208 [0.80, 1.25] (equal on log-scale to \u00b1log 1.25 \u2248 \u00b10.223).\n(ii) Tail comparability: call m tail-comparable iff the 95% bootstrap CIs of \u03b1_P(BAdam) and \u03b1_P(Adam) for m overlap.\nGlobal answer (mechanical):\n- YES: (A) median over m of \u03c1_m \u2208 [0.80, 1.25]; AND (B) \u2265 80% of modules rank-comparable; AND (C) \u2265 80% of modules tail-comparable.\n- NO: any of (A)/(B)/(C) fails; in that case also report which of the three conditions failed and by how much.\nReport all three conditions explicitly; the single-word verdict follows from the rule. Per-module-type medians, |\u0394\u03b1| distribution, and spiked-component counts (2.6/4) are reported as diagnostics, not as verdict inputs. Equivalence backup: TOST-style two-one-sided bounds on log \u03c1 with \u00b1log(1.25), reported per module type as descriptive p-values (modules are not independent i.i.d. samples, so this is corroborating only).\n\n2.5 Interpretation mapping: the research question's target \"comparable\" is exactly the conjunction in 2.4. A \"s\u00ed\" is the mechanical output of the rule; a \"no\" is reported with the failing component. No experiment is claimed to have produced either outcome here.\n\n2.6 Numerical-noise handling (for possibly small BAdam perturbations): For each module, estimate the noise-floor singular values via the Marc\u0306enko\u2013Pastur bulk: fit the MP law to the small-singular-value bulk using the module's aspect ratio and an empirical noise variance; \u03c3_edge = MP bulk edge. Count #spiked components above \u03c3_edge. If #spiked < 5 for a module, mark its \u03b1 and r_\u03c4 as low-confidence: EXCLUDE it from statistics (B) and (C) of the verdict but list it in an appendix table with its scale \u2016\u0394W\u2016_F. Additionally verify SVD stability by recomputing the largest ~50 singular values with double-reorthogonalized SVD and comparing to dense SVD (a sanity check, since some SVD packs under-estimate small singular values for thin matrices).\n\n# 3 Controls and comparison structure\n\n3.1 Fixed across arms (all runs): identical W_0 checkpoint (same random init from fixed seed s0, deep-copied into every arm; if a pretrained checkpoint were available it would be the same object for all arms), same tokenizer and fixed shuffled data order (deterministic RNG), same batches, same optimizer hyperparameters (AdamW: lr=2e-5, \u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999, \u03b5=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0 primary / 0.01 robustness sweep), same cosine schedule with 5% warmup over the SAME global step count T, same seq_len/batch/epochs, same validation split and metric (per-token cross-entropy). The only difference across arms is the optimizer's block structure.\n\n3.2 Randomness: weights seeded at s0 and identical across all arms; shuffle seed fixed; 5 experiment seeds {0,1,2,3,4} reproduce data-shuffle/tokenizer/batch randomness across arms (each seed runs ALL arms, giving paired same-seed observations). All CIs pooled over seeds and layers for type-level, and per-same-seed for module-level pairing.\n\n3.3 Adam vs BAdam grid (primary convention): arms = {Adam} \u222a {BAdam, K\u2208{2,4,12}} \u00d7 seeds {0..4} = 20 runs. BAdam blocks: contiguous layer groups that never split a layer: K=2 \u2192 layers {0-5},{6-11}; K=4 \u2192 {0-2},{3-5},{6-8},{9-11}; K=12 \u2192 one block per layer; blocks cyclically activated (one active block per global step using that block's Adam states; others frozen); per-layer modules always stay together.\n\n3.4 Primary convention \u2014 EQUAL FORWARD PASSES: T_Adam = T_Badam = T=733 global steps for all arms (same epochs, same schedule object over same global indices). Implication: each Adam module gets n_m \u2248 733 active solver steps; each BAdam module gets n_m \u2248 \u230a733/K\u230b (\u2248366 for K=2, 183 for K=4, 61 for K=12). This directly matches the BAdam paper's evaluation protocol (same data passes), and since the verdict metrics are scale-invariant, the expected fewer-active-step per-module effect is isolated as the \"block structure effect\" rather than a magnitude confound.\n3.5 Secondary convention \u2014 EQUAL PER-BLOCK ACTIVE STEPS (robustness): run BAdam for K\u00b7T global steps so every module receives n_m \u2248 T \u2248 733 active steps, i.e., the same number of applied Adam increments per module as in the Adam arm. This requires K\u00d7 more forward passes and a K\u00d7 longer schedule; we also run a \"per-cycle LR restart\" variant of the same schedule to decorrelate total-schedule-length from per-block update count. Report both primary and secondary verdicts; a conclusion is called stable iff primary and secondary agree under both conventions and under wd\u2208{0,0.01}.\n\n# 4 Analysis plan\n\n4.1 Per-run extraction: for every module m: \u0394W_m, n_m, \u2016\u0394W_m\u2016_F; SVD \u2192 {\u03b1_P, \u03b1_H, \u03b1_RF, their CIs}, {PR, r_0.9/d, r_0.99/d}, #spiked (MP bulk edge), scale \u2016\u0394W_m\u2016_F.\n4.2 Type-level table (the main deliverable): rows = module type \u2208 {q, k, v, o, up, gate, down}; columns = arm (Adam, BAdam K=2/4/12) \u00d7 descriptor: \u03b1_P (median, 95% bootstrap CI over layers & seeds), \u03b1_H, \u03b1_RF, PR, r_0.9/d, r_0.99/d, \u2016\u0394W\u2016_F, plus paired \u03c1_m and \u0394\u03b1 = \u03b1_B \u2212 \u03b1_A with CI, and #spiked. Same layout for primary and secondary conventions.\n4.3 Layer-depth curves: for each arm, r_0.9/d(\u2113) and \u03b1_P(\u2113) vs layer \u2113\u22080..11 (median and CI band over seeds), Adam and BAdam(K=4) overlaid; other K in appendix.\n4.4 Pairwise statistics: histogram/empirical-CDF of \u03c1_m and of |\u0394\u03b1| across the 84 modules; per-type TOST-equivalence summary; report the fraction failing each verdict condition.\n4.5 Sensitivity of the verdict: (i) re-fit \u03b1_P across x_min percentile choices (e.g., x_min \u2208 10th..90th percentile of tail) and recompute condition (C); (ii) r_\u03c4 with \u03c4\u2208{0.5, 0.9, 0.99}; (iii) wd\u2208{0, 0.01}; (iv) primary vs secondary convention; (v) #spiked threshold 3 vs 5. The verdict is reported as robust to (i)\u2013(v) or the specific sensitive axis is named.\n4.6 Appendix: low-confidence modules (per 2.6), embedding/lm_head spectral appendix, per-seed raw tables.\n\n# 5 Feasibility\n- Main cost is the finetuning, not the SVD. ~110M-param forward/backward at seq=256: \u2248 8\u201316 GFLOP/step; 733 steps/run \u2192 ~1e13\u20131.2e13 FLOP/run. On 224 CPU cores with a vectorized CPU torch build, estimate \u22480.5\u20132 h/run; primary grid 20 runs fully parallelizable \u2192 \u224810\u201340 CPU-core-hours total (a few hours wall-clock at this core count). Secondary grid (K\u00b7T with T=733) \u2248 3\u00d7 more steps aggregate.\n- SVD per module is negligible: largest matrix 768\u00d72048 \u2192 O(768\u00b2\u00b72048) \u2248 1.2 GFLOP \u2248 <1 s on CPU; 84 modules + appendix \u2248 <2 min/run. Memory: model ~110M params \u2248 0.5 GB fp32; block-wise optimizer states \u2248 full-Adam states only for the active block \u2248 small footprint.\n- Environment note: this sandbox has Python 3.14.4 but NO numpy/torch/transformers installed and network is disabled, so a real run REQUIRES installing dependencies (torch, transformers or a minimal custom LLaMA forward/backward in pure torch/numpy) and providing the local corpus file \u2014 explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for this design task. No results are claimed; this is a design-only deliverable.\n\n# 6 Uncertainties and how they are mitigated/reported\n- U1 \u2014 \u03b1 sensitivity to tail selection (x_min/k). Mitigations: primary = CSN x_min via KS; report \u03b1_H plateau and \u03b1_RF; run the x_min sweep (4.5i) and state whether the verdict is stable.\n- U2 \u2014 Definition of \"equality\" Adam vs BAdam (equal epochs vs equal per-block steps). Mitigations: pre-register BOTH conventions (primary=equal forward passes per BAdam paper protocol; secondary=equal per-block active steps); report both; conclusion \"stable iff\" both agree.\n- U3 \u2014 Scaling from BAdam's paper conventions (LLaMA-2-7B layer-range blocks) to our K\u2208{2,4,12} layer-group blocks. Mitigations: contiguous layer groups not splitting layers mirror the paper's block structure; K sweep covers coarse\u2192per-layer granularity; clearly labeled as a scale-down analog, not a replication.\n- U4 \u2014 Numerical noise for very small \u0394W (SVD under-estimates tiny singular values; BAdam may yield smaller per-module displacement). Mitigations: MP bulk-edge spiked-component counting, low-confidence module flagging/exclusion, dense-SVD vs reorthogonalized-SVD sanity check, and scale-free metrics (\u03b1, PR, r/d) that do not conflate magnitude with shape.\n- U5 \u2014 Module non-independence (84 modules share layers/seeds) makes classic hypothesis tests optimistic. Mitigations: verdict uses medians & CI overlap, not i.i.d. assumptions; TOST reported as descriptive only; pairing is same-seed same-module.\n- U6 \u2014 Corpus/model are not BAdam's original LLaMA-7B/instruction data; the design targets structural (shape) comparability which is scale-invariant, and any limitation to generalizing beyond our 110M/1M-token setup is stated as such.\n- Assumption list (fixed requirements vs choices): fixed = answer the yes/no question with pre-registered rules; design-only; CPU budget constraints (\u2264~125M model, small token budget). Choices (can be revised) = tiny-LLaMA-110M, Wiki-2-style 1M-token slice, K\u2208{2,4,12}, T=733, 5 seeds, thresholds \u03c1\u2208[0.8,1.25]/80% overlap, CSN \u03b1_P as primary tail estimator.", "evidence": "Exploration: /workspace contains only task.json (question + 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'); no BAdam source, checkpoints, or datasets anywhere; Python 3.14.4 with numpy/torch/transformers/scipy/sklearn all failing to import; network disabled; no GPU; 224 CPUs / ~2TB RAM. Design is therefore source-grounded, not artifact-grounded: BAdam (Luo et al., arXiv:2404.02827 \u2014 block-coordinate / block-Adam finetuning with per-block Adam states and cyclic block activation); AdamW (Kingma & Ba, 2015; Loshchilov & Hutter, 2019 \u2014 additive per-step updates \u21d2 \u0394W = W_T \u2212 W_0 per module); heavy-tailed spectra & RMT (Martin & Mahoney, JMLR 2021 / arXiv:1901.08278; Marc\u0306enko\u2013Pastur bulk edge); Hill (1975) estimator; Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman (SIAM Review 2009) power-law MLE with x_min; participation ratio and r_\u03c4 threshold rank (effective dimensionality, cf. Roy & Vetterli 2007). Parameter and FLOP estimates above are arithmetic checks on the chosen config (110M params; 733 steps \u00d7 ~1e10 FLOP/step; SVD flops O(d_min\u00b2\u00b7d_max)).", "assumptions": ["Design-only: no execution, no simulated outcomes.", "Same model, init, data order, schedule, and epochs across optimizer arms; only block structure differs.", "LLaMA-style 110M decoder and 1M-token LM slice are a faithful, CPU-feasible scale-down analog of BAdam's LLaMA settings.", "Additive updates make \u0394W_m = W_T - W_0 the natural per-module learning perturbation.", "Shape metrics (\u03b1, PR, r/d) are scale-invariant, decoupling structural comparability from expected BAdam/Adam magnitude differences.", "Comparability rule is pre-registered and mechanical (median \u03c1 in [0.8,1.25], \u226580% module rank-overlap, \u226580% tail-CI overlap).", "A real run would need installing torch/transformers and a local corpus file (out of scope here)."], "uncertainties": ["\u03b1 sensitivity to tail-length/x_min selection \u2192 CSN x_min + Hill plateau + rank-frequency OLS + explicit x_min sweep.", "Definition of 'equal' Adam vs BAdam \u2192 pre-register primary (equal forward passes) and secondary (equal per-block active steps) conventions; stable verdict requires agreement.", "Scaling from BAdam's LLaMA-scale layer-range blocks to K={2,4,12} layer groups \u2192 contiguous not-splitting-layers blocks, flagged as analog.", "SVD numerical noise for small perturbations \u2192 MP bulk-edge spiked counting, low-confidence exclusion, dual-SVD sanity check.", "Module non-independence \u2192 verdict via medians/CI-overlap; TOST descriptive only.", "Generalization beyond 110M/1M-token setup stated as a limit."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bb4090ea0bd949a0a61c2556ca58bc66", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Provide complete BAdam-vs-Adam spectral comparison experiment design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
